11.2  setuid, setgid and sticky bit

TL;DR
  - setuid (s in the owner's x spot): the program runs with its owner's rights,
    not yours. That's how passwd can update /etc/shadow.
  - setgid on a program: it runs with the file's group. On a folder: new files
    get the folder's group.
  - Sticky bit (t on a folder): only a file's owner can delete it, even where
    everyone may write. /tmp has it.

      ls -ld /usr/bin/passwd /tmp
      chmod g+s shared/       # new files inherit the folder's group

Pitfalls
  - A capital S or T means the bit is set but the x under it isn't: usually a
    mistake.
  - Linux ignores setuid on scripts (shell, Python…): it only works on compiled
    programs.
  - setuid root programs are what attackers look for. List them with
    find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null and know why each is there.
  - A filesystem mounted with nosuid (common for USB drives and /tmp) ignores
    these bits.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 11.2 --why

Related
  Commands  chmod, getcap
  Paths     /usr/bin/passwd, /tmp
  See also  idk 11.1 rwx, octal and umask
  See also  idk 9.2 /tmp vs /var/tmp
